   "source": [
    "# AI101 Assignment 4 - Task 1 Financial Data\n\n**Purpose:** Use Gemini to analyze synthetic company financial data and predict stock prices.\n\n**Dataset note:** This project uses a small synthetic dataset created for practice. It is not real financial or vehicle-market data. The AI-generated predictions are demonstrations, not professional advice.\n\n## 1. Install and import libraries\nThe first cell installs Google's current Gemini Python SDK (`google-genai`). The remaining imports handle tables, files, and graphs."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "6f2055ba",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "!pip -q install google-genai pandas matplotlib"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "efbbb470",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import os\nimport re\nimport io\nimport getpass\nimport pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom google import genai\n\n# In Colab, add GEMINI_API_KEY under the Secrets panel to avoid retyping it.\n# If no Colab secret or environment variable is set, prompt privately.\napi_key = os.environ.get(\"GEMINI_API_KEY\")\nif not api_key:\n    try:\n        from google.colab import userdata\n        api_key = userdata.get(\"GEMINI_API_KEY\")\n    except Exception:\n        api_key = None\nif not api_key:\n    api_key = getpass.getpass(\"Enter your Gemini API key: \")\n\nclient = genai.Client(api_key=api_key)\nMODEL_NAME = \"gemini-3.5-flash-lite\"\n\n# Quick connection check requested by the assignment handout.\ndemo_response = client.models.generate_content(\n    model=MODEL_NAME,\n    contents=\"What is the capital of France?\"\n)\nprint(demo_response.text)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f87b9cb0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Load the CSV dataset\nIn Colab, upload only `finance_data.csv` from the repository's `data` folder when prompted. The notebook stops with a clear error if a different file is selected, then reads the CSV into a pandas DataFrame. A DataFrame is a table that Python can analyze."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "f2b0990c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from google.colab import files\n\nuploaded = files.upload()  # Select only finance_data.csv.\nfile_path = \"finance_data.csv\"\n\n# Stop clearly if the expected dataset was not uploaded.\nif file_path not in uploaded:\n    raise FileNotFoundError(\"Upload finance_data.csv from the repository's data folder.\")\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(file_path)\nprint(\"Dataset preview:\")\ndisplay(df.head())\nprint(\"\\n",
    "Dataset dimensions:\", df.shape)\nprint(\"\\n",
    "Column information:\")\ndf.info()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d585b345",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Ask Gemini to analyze the data\nThis prompt gives Gemini the entire CSV and asks for trends, metrics, and observations. The output is natural-language analysis rather than a prediction table. The full dataset, including its target column, is included for analysis only."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "437f9363",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "csv_string = df.to_csv(index=False)\n\nanalysis_prompt = f'''\nAnalyze the following dataset in CSV format.\nExplain the main trends, important metrics, relationships between\ncolumns, and any unusual observations. Use specific examples from\nthe data. Do not claim that correlation proves causation.\nState that the dataset is synthetic.\n\nDATA:\n{csv_string}\n'''\n\nanalysis_response = client.models.generate_content(\\n",
    "    model=MODEL_NAME, contents=analysis_prompt\\n",
    ")\nprint(analysis_response.text)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6f36919e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The next prompt tells Gemini which column is the target and uses both `company_id` and `date` to identify each row. Company IDs repeat across dates, so both fields are required for an unambiguous match. The prompt requests machine-readable CSV output so it can be loaded into pandas."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "c0cd086d",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Exclude the actual target from model input to prevent target leakage.\nprediction_csv = df.drop(columns=[\"stock_price\"]).to_csv(index=False)\n\nprediction_prompt = f'''\nYou are analyzing a synthetic practice dataset.\nPredict the value of the \"stock_price\" column for every row using\nthe other available columns. Do not use the actual \"stock_price\"\nvalue as an input when making the prediction.\nKeep each row matched to its original \"company_id\" AND \"date\" because each company appears on multiple dates.\nReturn exactly three columns named \"company_id\", \"date\", and \"predicted_stock_price\".\nReturn one prediction for every input row.\nOutput ONLY valid CSV data: no explanation, no Markdown fences.\n\nDATA:\n{csv_string}\n'''\n\nprediction_response = client.models.generate_content(\\n",
    "    model=MODEL_NAME, contents=prediction_prompt\\n",
    ")\nraw_prediction_text = prediction_response.text\nprint(raw_prediction_text)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "65d47f55",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Convert Gemini's CSV response into a DataFrame\nThis code removes optional Markdown fences, skips any leading explanation, and parses the CSV header and rows with pandas. It normalizes the two expected columns, converts the prediction to a number, and checks for duplicate, missing, or extra identifiers. A validated one-to-one merge then attaches predictions to the original rows. If Gemini omits or duplicates a row, the notebook stops with a clear error instead of silently reporting incomplete results."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "803e5431",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def response_to_dataframe(response_text):\n    # Remove Markdown code fences if Gemini includes them.\n    cleaned = response_text.strip()\n    cleaned = re.sub(r\"^```(?:csv)?\\s*\", \"\", cleaned, flags=re.IGNORECASE)\n    cleaned = re.sub(r\"\\s*```$\", \"\", cleaned)\n\n    # Keep the response starting at the expected CSV header.\n    lines = cleaned.splitlines()\n    header_index = None\n    for i, line in enumerate(lines):\n        if \"company_id\" in line and \"predicted_\" in line:\n            header_index = i\n            break\n    if header_index is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"Could not find the expected CSV header in Gemini's response.\")\n\n    csv_text = \"\\n",
    "\".join(lines[header_index:])\n    return pd.read_csv(io.StringIO(csv_text))\n\npredictions = response_to_dataframe(raw_prediction_text)\npredictions.columns = predictions.columns.str.strip()\nexpected_prediction_column = \"predicted_stock_price\"\nkey_columns = [\"company_id\", \"date\"]\n\n# Match the identifier, date, and prediction columns even if capitalization varies.\ncolumn_lookup = {c.lower(): c for c in predictions.columns}\nif any(c not in column_lookup for c in key_columns + [expected_prediction_column]):\n    raise ValueError(\"Expected columns: company_id, date, and predicted_stock_price\")\nsource_columns = [column_lookup[c] for c in key_columns]\npred_match = column_lookup[expected_prediction_column]\npredictions = predictions[source_columns + [pred_match]].rename(\n    columns={**dict(zip(source_columns, key_columns)), pred_match: expected_prediction_column}\n)\npredictions[\"company_id\"] = predictions[\"company_id\"].astype(str).str.strip()\npredictions[\"date\"] = predictions[\"date\"].astype(str).str.strip()\npredictions[expected_prediction_column] = pd.to_numeric(\n    predictions[expected_prediction_column], errors=\"coerce\"\n)\npredictions = predictions.dropna(subset=[expected_prediction_column])\n\n# A company appears on several dates, so the two-column key must be unique.\nif predictions.duplicated(subset=key_columns).any():\n    raise ValueError(\"Gemini returned duplicate company_id/date pairs.\")\nsource_keys = set(map(tuple, df[key_columns].astype(str).to_numpy()))\nprediction_keys = set(map(tuple, predictions[key_columns].to_numpy()))\nmissing_keys = source_keys - prediction_keys\nextra_keys = prediction_keys - source_keys\nif missing_keys or extra_keys or len(predictions) != len(df):\n    raise ValueError(f\"Prediction rows do not match the source data. Missing: {missing_keys}; extra: {extra_keys}\")\n\n# Join by both company and date to attach each estimate to the correct observation.\nresults = df.merge(predictions, on=key_columns, how=\"left\", validate=\"one_to_one\")\nif results[expected_prediction_column].isna().any():\n    raise ValueError(\"Some source rows do not have a valid prediction.\")\ndisplay(results.head())\nprint(\"Rows with predictions:\", len(results))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "78a3ad95",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. Compare actual values with AI predictions\nThe table compares the original target column with Gemini's predicted column. The mean absolute error (MAE) is the average absolute difference between actual and predicted values. Lower MAE means closer predictions on this dataset."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "a3147ffc",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "results[\"absolute_error\"] = (\n    results[\"stock_price\"] - results[\"predicted_stock_price\"]\n).abs()\n\nprint(\"Mean absolute error:\", round(results[\"absolute_error\"].mean(), 2))\ndisplay(results[[\"company_id\", \"stock_price\", \"predicted_stock_price\", \"absolute_error\"]])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "41a61eeb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 7. Plot actual versus predicted values\nA separate chart panel is drawn for each company, with date on the horizontal axis and actual and predicted prices on the vertical axis. This preserves each company's monthly sequence and makes overestimates or underestimates easy to see."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "9deccdf6",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "company_ids = sorted(results[\"company_id\"].astype(str).unique())\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(len(company_ids), 1, figsize=(12, 4 * len(company_ids)), squeeze=False)\n\n# Draw a separate date-based comparison for each company.\nfor ax, company_id in zip(axes[:, 0], company_ids):\n    company_data = results[results[\"company_id\"].astype(str) == company_id].sort_values(\"date\")\n    ax.plot(company_data[\"date\"], company_data[\"stock_price\"],\n            marker=\"o\", label=\"Actual stock_price\")\n    ax.plot(company_data[\"date\"], company_data[\"predicted_stock_price\"],\n            marker=\"x\", label=\"AI predicted stock_price\")\n    ax.set_title(company_id)\n    ax.set_xlabel(\"date\")\n    ax.set_ylabel(\"stock_price\")\n    ax.tick_params(axis=\"x\", rotation=45)\n    ax.legend()\n\nfig.suptitle(\"Actual vs. AI-Predicted stock_price by Company\", y=1.01)\nfig.tight_layout()\nplt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
